00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:30.940
So the fundamentals
of billing on Cloud.

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:33.840
Let's start with projects.

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:40.950
Projects are the core
organizing entity in Cloud.

00:05:40.950 --> 00:05:44.370
When you have a service such as
Compute Engine, or Kubernetes,

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:51.060
or Cloud SQL, or Storage, you'll
see them creating resources

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:54.242
in the context of projects.

00:05:54.242 --> 00:05:57.310
Now, projects are
containers for resources,

00:05:57.310 --> 00:06:01.000
and also describe the
settings and permissions

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:04.630
and other metadata
around your workloads.

00:06:07.640 --> 00:06:12.700
A separate concept in Cloud is
that of the billing account.

00:06:12.700 --> 00:06:14.830
And a billing account
defines who pays

00:06:14.830 --> 00:06:17.430
for projects and resources.

00:06:17.430 --> 00:06:23.590
Now, a project is always
paid for by a single billing

00:06:23.590 --> 00:06:25.050
account.

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:28.350
And billing accounts actually
have their own separate roles

00:06:28.350 --> 00:06:33.580
and permissions from
projects in the hierarchy.

00:06:33.580 --> 00:06:36.370
A billing account
operates in one currency.

00:06:36.370 --> 00:06:40.300
And it can be funded by a
credit card, a debit card,

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:43.810
other payment instruments,
depending on jurisdiction,

00:06:43.810 --> 00:06:46.880
or it can be funded
by an invoice.

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:52.940
Billing accounts are always
funded on a monthly basis.

00:06:52.940 --> 00:06:55.210
Now, it's easy-- in fact,
it's incredibly easy

00:06:55.210 --> 00:06:57.940
to move projects between
billing accounts.

00:06:57.940 --> 00:07:00.270
You can do it
yourself, and you can

00:07:00.270 --> 00:07:08.260
self-serve that process using
the billing section of Cloud.

00:07:08.260 --> 00:07:10.540
One request-- if we could
reload the presentation.

00:07:10.540 --> 00:07:12.230
I've noticed we're
on an older version,

00:07:12.230 --> 00:07:14.172
so if you could just
hit reload in the middle

00:07:14.172 --> 00:07:16.630
on the same screen, I'm sure
it'll come up with the latest.

00:07:21.450 --> 00:07:26.830
So in addition to projects,
we have both organizations

00:07:26.830 --> 00:07:30.850
and folders, which are ways
of containing and grouping

00:07:30.850 --> 00:07:33.322
projects.

00:07:33.322 --> 00:07:35.030
The best way to think
of these structures

00:07:35.030 --> 00:07:37.350
is that they mirror
your organization.

00:07:37.350 --> 00:07:41.150
That's why we named
organizations Organizations.

00:07:41.150 --> 00:07:44.120
And folders can
be nested, and you

00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:46.700
can think of them as the
primary key, in a sense,

00:07:46.700 --> 00:07:47.795
to your projects.

00:07:47.795 --> 00:07:53.010
Now, a project can only live
and be owned by one folder.

00:07:53.010 --> 00:07:56.310
Organizations can also
contain billing accounts,

00:07:56.310 --> 00:07:59.850
allowing for the central
management of billing roles

00:07:59.850 --> 00:08:02.580
and permissions, but they're
independent from projects.

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:05.364
They have separate roles,
separate permissions.

00:08:09.630 --> 00:08:15.340
Finally, at the most granular
level, we have labels.

00:08:15.340 --> 00:08:20.110
Labels are ad hoc, key value
pairs assigned to resources.

00:08:24.970 --> 00:08:25.470
It is.

00:08:25.470 --> 00:08:26.940
We just have to
hit reload on it.

00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:28.160
We'll be all set.

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:29.095
All right.

00:08:37.020 --> 00:08:37.770
Thanks.

00:08:37.770 --> 00:08:39.570
We'll pick it back
up with labels.

00:08:39.570 --> 00:08:42.735
Labels associate resources
across projects and

00:08:42.735 --> 00:08:45.240
are the crosscutting ways of
doing reporting that you can't

00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:47.722
model with nested folders.

00:08:47.722 --> 00:08:48.930
So a number of examples here.

00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:52.320
What team is working on
a particular resource?

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:53.610
Is it a database?

00:08:53.610 --> 00:08:54.540
Is it in production?

00:08:54.540 --> 00:08:55.740
Is it in Mountain View?

00:08:55.740 --> 00:08:57.030
Is it active?

00:08:57.030 --> 00:08:58.440
Is it pending?

00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:03.280
You can apply those key
value pairs across resources.

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:07.170
And additionally, they may have
other powers on a per service

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:08.154
basis.

00:09:08.154 --> 00:09:10.320
But for billing, they're a
tremendously powerful way

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:11.640
to crosscut reporting.

00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:16.770
And critically, they appear in
your BigQuery billing export--

00:09:16.770 --> 00:09:19.377
a topic you will hear
me keep hammering on

00:09:19.377 --> 00:09:21.210
until I've convinced
every single one of you

00:09:21.210 --> 00:09:22.626
that it's a wonderful
thing to do.

00:09:25.810 --> 00:09:29.250
Now, cost management is
how we report and allow you

00:09:29.250 --> 00:09:33.750
to control those costs across
all of your projects, folders,

00:09:33.750 --> 00:09:36.242
and within your organization.

00:09:36.242 --> 00:09:37.950
Now, I'm going to draw
a distinction here

00:09:37.950 --> 00:09:42.420
between your invoice,
which tells you how much,

00:09:42.420 --> 00:09:46.050
and cost management, which
tells you why and where costs

00:09:46.050 --> 00:09:46.860
were raised.

00:09:46.860 --> 00:09:51.375
And we'll look in a moment
at how those differ.

00:09:51.375 --> 00:09:53.000
Cost management lets
you slice and dice

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:57.440
cost, particularly by
project, but also by label,

00:09:57.440 --> 00:10:00.650
and over time, folder.

00:10:00.650 --> 00:10:03.734
And the major areas are
cost reporting, budgets

00:10:03.734 --> 00:10:05.150
and alerting, to
let you know when

00:10:05.150 --> 00:10:10.790
you've hit certain thresholds
of cost, and our famed export.

00:10:10.790 --> 00:10:13.460
There are four other sessions
with a lot more detail that you

00:10:13.460 --> 00:10:15.560
may like to attend later on--

00:10:15.560 --> 00:10:18.560
monitoring and forecasting,
saving money on Compute

00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:22.470
Engine-- a topic worthy
of its own discussion--

00:10:22.470 --> 00:10:25.770
a deep dive into exporting
data, and cutting and slicing it

00:10:25.770 --> 00:10:29.890
with Data Studio, and a
financial governance session

00:10:29.890 --> 00:10:32.690
about controlling cost.

00:10:32.690 --> 00:10:35.000
Now first big, green tip.

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:37.172
Of course, you already
knew what it would be,

00:10:37.172 --> 00:10:38.630
and we'll come back
to it later on.

00:10:38.630 --> 00:10:40.790
It's turn on the
BigQuery billing export.

00:10:40.790 --> 00:10:45.230
Now, this export exports
extremely fine grain data

00:10:45.230 --> 00:10:48.110
about your usage of Cloud.

00:10:48.110 --> 00:10:51.830
But even at high volume, it's
unlikely to cost you very much

00:10:51.830 --> 00:10:53.120
to turn on this export.

00:10:53.120 --> 00:10:57.410
Your free cap includes some
storage that will likely

00:10:57.410 --> 00:10:59.365
cover your use of this export.

00:10:59.365 --> 00:11:00.740
But we let you
turn it on, so you

00:11:00.740 --> 00:11:06.422
have control of both access to
it and what project it sits in.

00:11:06.422 --> 00:11:08.880
And you can turn it on in the
billing section under Billing

00:11:08.880 --> 00:11:12.840
Export, choose a project
that it resides in,

00:11:12.840 --> 00:11:15.610
and choose a data
set to host it in.

00:11:15.610 --> 00:11:18.820
So that's tip number one.

00:11:18.820 --> 00:11:22.400
So let's get started with
our Hello, World example.

00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:27.160
We have one billing account,
one project, and five services

00:11:27.160 --> 00:11:28.370
that we're funding.

00:11:28.370 --> 00:11:31.600
So a billing account
pays for a project.

00:11:31.600 --> 00:11:34.150
Project owns resources.

00:11:34.150 --> 00:11:37.510
And let's flip our colors so we
have a little color coded key

00:11:37.510 --> 00:11:39.640
for the rest of this
discussion, and note

00:11:39.640 --> 00:11:45.260
that the pays for is the
dotted green line, and owns

00:11:45.260 --> 00:11:46.860
is the blue line.

00:11:46.860 --> 00:11:49.990
Billing accounts don't own
projects, they just fund them

00:11:49.990 --> 00:11:51.580
and that's an
important distinction

00:11:51.580 --> 00:11:56.010
as we go through our
discussion of ownership.

00:11:56.010 --> 00:11:59.040
So we've just started
our new company--

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:01.560
one billing account,
one production project.

00:12:01.560 --> 00:12:02.550
Things are great.

00:12:02.550 --> 00:12:03.720
Users are pouring in.

00:12:03.720 --> 00:12:05.770
We've got all our resources
under the hood there.

00:12:05.770 --> 00:12:08.080
I've hidden them so that
it's a little bit easier

00:12:08.080 --> 00:12:10.080
to read these slides, but
under that project are

00:12:10.080 --> 00:12:13.372
all of the resources in GCP.

00:12:13.372 --> 00:12:14.330
Things are going great.

00:12:14.330 --> 00:12:15.496
We've hired two developers--

00:12:15.496 --> 00:12:17.070
Alice and Bob.

00:12:17.070 --> 00:12:18.650
Alice and Bob are
off to the races.

00:12:18.650 --> 00:12:19.649
They're staying up late.

00:12:19.649 --> 00:12:23.640
They're working hard to get
us that next set of users.

00:12:23.640 --> 00:12:28.430
Unfortunately, Bob is not the
world's greatest Linux hacker.

00:12:28.430 --> 00:12:31.580
He doesn't realize that rm -rf
works quickly and efficiently

00:12:31.580 --> 00:12:33.590
to destroy your files.

00:12:33.590 --> 00:12:35.699
Alice is a really fast typist.

00:12:35.699 --> 00:12:37.490
But every now and then,
she forgets a where

00:12:37.490 --> 00:12:40.130
clause in her SQL statements.

00:12:40.130 --> 00:12:41.990
Now, I'll do my
own show of hands.

00:12:41.990 --> 00:12:45.230
In my time before Google, I
have done both of these things

00:12:45.230 --> 00:12:46.550
on large production systems.

00:12:46.550 --> 00:12:50.630
I ran an uncapped where clause
on a 100 million row table.

00:12:50.630 --> 00:12:52.850
Caught that a couple
million rows in.

00:12:52.850 --> 00:12:55.760
I have deleted the root file
system of a production Linux

00:12:55.760 --> 00:12:56.870
host.

00:12:56.870 --> 00:12:58.850
It's always the
pause that gets you.

00:12:58.850 --> 00:13:01.020
You sit and you think, why
is this taking so long?

00:13:01.020 --> 00:13:01.520
Oh.

00:13:04.100 --> 00:13:06.140
Another tip along the way--

00:13:06.140 --> 00:13:11.230
you can change the radius
of the smile in the faces

00:13:11.230 --> 00:13:12.470
on Google Slides.

00:13:12.470 --> 00:13:14.470
So I highly recommend you
explore those advanced

00:13:14.470 --> 00:13:14.969
features.

00:13:14.969 --> 00:13:18.330
[LAUGHTER]

00:13:18.330 --> 00:13:21.750
So from Hello, World to hello,
development environment.

00:13:21.750 --> 00:13:24.869
Why on Earth would we run
everything in one project,

00:13:24.869 --> 00:13:26.160
give everyone permission to it?

00:13:26.160 --> 00:13:28.350
Of course, it's best
practice to carve

00:13:28.350 --> 00:13:32.190
out environments, to isolate
environments by stage

00:13:32.190 --> 00:13:33.600
and by who's using them.

00:13:33.600 --> 00:13:35.880
So we forgive Alice and Bob.

00:13:35.880 --> 00:13:37.490
We have a blameless post-mortem.

00:13:37.490 --> 00:13:39.240
We should have offered
better protections.

00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:40.073
They're still happy.

00:13:40.073 --> 00:13:44.340
They're still working away,
not running terrible removes

00:13:44.340 --> 00:13:47.100
and updates in production.

00:13:47.100 --> 00:13:49.810
Now how will you see this cost?

00:13:49.810 --> 00:13:51.820
Well, we'll talk about
that in a moment.

00:13:51.820 --> 00:13:53.940
But this brings
our second tip, one

00:13:53.940 --> 00:13:56.700
that I think will resonate
well with users of GCP.

00:13:56.700 --> 00:13:59.100
Use projects to
isolate and enforce

00:13:59.100 --> 00:14:03.030
boundaries between
resources and use them

00:14:03.030 --> 00:14:05.220
as containers for policy.

00:14:05.220 --> 00:14:07.512
So they enforce
IAM permissions--

00:14:07.512 --> 00:14:08.970
access and management
permissions--

00:14:08.970 --> 00:14:13.620
of who can access resources and
other policies to dictate what

00:14:13.620 --> 00:14:19.430
happens in a particular subset
of your resources on GCP.

00:14:19.430 --> 00:14:21.760
So you should create
projects freely.

00:14:21.760 --> 00:14:24.070
Lots of customers in GCP
start with one project

00:14:24.070 --> 00:14:26.260
and go all the way
up to thousands.

00:14:26.260 --> 00:14:30.890
You can think of them as
almost a free construct.

00:14:30.890 --> 00:14:32.510
So how do these charges appear?

00:14:32.510 --> 00:14:34.910
Well, you'll get a bill,
something like this

00:14:34.910 --> 00:14:37.100
every month, an invoice.

00:14:37.100 --> 00:14:40.310
And it tells you, of course,
how much is due up at the top.

00:14:40.310 --> 00:14:43.400
It also tells you, on
a per project basis,

00:14:43.400 --> 00:14:46.500
the usage of each service.

00:14:46.500 --> 00:14:49.230
Now, a couple of
notes on this invoice.

00:14:49.230 --> 00:14:53.110
First, the invoice is
always at the project level.

00:14:53.110 --> 00:14:55.050
It is not going to
cut down and slice up

00:14:55.050 --> 00:14:58.860
your cost into finer buckets.

00:14:58.860 --> 00:15:02.370
Second is that once
you hit 25 pages,

00:15:02.370 --> 00:15:06.240
this beautiful PDF turns
back into a one page PDF

00:15:06.240 --> 00:15:09.090
and says the number you owe.

00:15:09.090 --> 00:15:11.520
Some of our customers would
have literally thousands

00:15:11.520 --> 00:15:13.770
and thousands of
pages of invoice,

00:15:13.770 --> 00:15:15.630
and there's only
so much we're going

00:15:15.630 --> 00:15:18.220
to generate into PDF form.

00:15:18.220 --> 00:15:22.350
But behind every
PDF is also a CSV

00:15:22.350 --> 00:15:27.270
that shows this level of
detail for as many rows of data

00:15:27.270 --> 00:15:27.840
as you have.

00:15:31.330 --> 00:15:34.871
Cost reporting is the second way
you can surface this cost data.

00:15:34.871 --> 00:15:37.120
And there's a session that
goes much deeper into this.

00:15:37.120 --> 00:15:39.610
But cost reporting is the
place to slice and dice

00:15:39.610 --> 00:15:44.470
usage by time, by project,
by product, by SKU.

00:15:44.470 --> 00:15:47.410
You can slice and dice just
about any way you can imagine.

00:15:47.410 --> 00:15:49.720
And we're investing
a lot of resource

00:15:49.720 --> 00:15:53.445
into making this a fast, first
class way of understanding

00:15:53.445 --> 00:15:54.445
what's happening in GCP.

00:15:58.880 --> 00:16:00.740
We'll come to a tip
in a moment, but this

00:16:00.740 --> 00:16:03.320
is the place to
look for why cost

00:16:03.320 --> 00:16:06.197
is happening and from where.

00:16:06.197 --> 00:16:07.780
Now, if you want to
go a level deeper,

00:16:07.780 --> 00:16:09.279
let's get into that
BigQuery export.

00:16:09.279 --> 00:16:10.630
What do you get?

00:16:10.630 --> 00:16:16.560
You get incredibly fine
grained usage, down to the SKU

00:16:16.560 --> 00:16:18.660
and label level of
everything that's

00:16:18.660 --> 00:16:21.600
happening across your projects.

00:16:21.600 --> 00:16:26.040
And we'll talk, when we look
at the features of labels,

00:16:26.040 --> 00:16:28.590
about how you can actually
use this to query your data.

00:16:28.590 --> 00:16:30.298
And of course, there's
a separate section

00:16:30.298 --> 00:16:31.540
that goes deep into it.

00:16:31.540 --> 00:16:34.450
But the BigQuery export
is a fantastic tool

00:16:34.450 --> 00:16:38.460
for understanding
every nuance of cost.

00:16:38.460 --> 00:16:40.950
Critically, when you turn
on your BigQuery export

00:16:40.950 --> 00:16:43.020
and have a question
down the road,

00:16:43.020 --> 00:16:45.030
you can grant access
to your export

00:16:45.030 --> 00:16:46.950
to Cloud Billing Support.

00:16:46.950 --> 00:16:48.700
We have a dedicated team--

00:16:48.700 --> 00:16:53.190
a support team that can help
you answer gnarly questions,

00:16:53.190 --> 00:16:56.040
and they can do it
alongside you in data that's

00:16:56.040 --> 00:16:59.660
available to both you and them.

00:16:59.660 --> 00:17:03.200
So our tip around cost
reporting and invoicing.

00:17:03.200 --> 00:17:06.349
The invoice tells
you what you owe.

00:17:06.349 --> 00:17:09.530
Cost reporting tells
you why and where.

00:17:09.530 --> 00:17:11.960
And we believe it's
important to keep

00:17:11.960 --> 00:17:15.290
those two concepts separate.

00:17:15.290 --> 00:17:18.859
So we've isolated our
resources into projects

00:17:18.859 --> 00:17:22.560
as the basic organizing
entity in GCP.

00:17:22.560 --> 00:17:26.760
We've looked at some of the
cost management tools and ways

00:17:26.760 --> 00:17:29.940
that cost has surfaced
across the platform.

00:17:29.940 --> 00:17:33.166
And the use cases are, of
course, much more broad

00:17:33.166 --> 00:17:34.290
than what I described here.

00:17:34.290 --> 00:17:36.414
With the production
environment and the development

00:17:36.414 --> 00:17:38.910
environment, you can cut your
projects any way you like,

00:17:38.910 --> 00:17:40.890
depending on how you
structure your resources.

00:17:40.890 --> 00:17:44.280
And we'll look at some
more ways in a moment.

00:17:44.280 --> 00:17:47.060
So our company is
growing like gangbusters.

00:17:47.060 --> 00:17:48.110
We have projects galore.

00:17:48.110 --> 00:17:50.420
We have added a staging
environment in the middle--

00:17:50.420 --> 00:17:52.700
also a great idea.

00:17:52.700 --> 00:17:54.510
We have an old macro-service.

00:17:54.510 --> 00:17:55.240
I made that up.

00:17:55.240 --> 00:17:56.281
It's not a micro-service.

00:17:56.281 --> 00:17:59.874
It's a monolith that we
forklift into a project.

00:17:59.874 --> 00:18:01.040
We have a TopSecret project.

00:18:01.040 --> 00:18:02.660
We're not sure what the
new business development

00:18:02.660 --> 00:18:05.120
team is doing, but they're
doing something in there--

00:18:05.120 --> 00:18:06.560
very locked down, very secret.

00:18:06.560 --> 00:18:07.580
Don't mention it.

00:18:07.580 --> 00:18:11.550
And an archive project where
we put old storage data

00:18:11.550 --> 00:18:15.680
in Cloud Storage.

00:18:15.680 --> 00:18:19.460
Now, you can imagine, as
you go from one to two

00:18:19.460 --> 00:18:23.990
to six to 10 projects to
100 that at some point,

00:18:23.990 --> 00:18:26.750
this becomes a
little bit painful.

00:18:26.750 --> 00:18:29.024
You might want to
control who has access

00:18:29.024 --> 00:18:30.440
to these at the
highest level, who

00:18:30.440 --> 00:18:33.440
is a member of your
organization, what policies do

00:18:33.440 --> 00:18:35.976
you want to flow down across
all of these projects.

00:18:35.976 --> 00:18:38.600
You might want to have detailed
audit logs of everything that's

00:18:38.600 --> 00:18:41.520
happening inside
this organization.

00:18:41.520 --> 00:18:45.020
And so we add a
centralization above projects

00:18:45.020 --> 00:18:47.642
called a GCP organization.

00:18:47.642 --> 00:18:48.350
And many of you--

00:18:48.350 --> 00:18:50.510
I think almost half the
room raised their hand

00:18:50.510 --> 00:18:55.570
when we asked how many of
you had a GCP organization.

00:18:55.570 --> 00:18:59.340
Now, I'm going to get rid
of those dotted green lines

00:18:59.340 --> 00:19:01.230
because something we're
working on hard today

00:19:01.230 --> 00:19:04.710
is to add the concept of
a default billing account

00:19:04.710 --> 00:19:06.062
at the organization level.

00:19:06.062 --> 00:19:07.770
So in the future, you
won't have to think

00:19:07.770 --> 00:19:13.460
about associating each project
to a separate billing account.

00:19:13.460 --> 00:19:16.160
So going forward, I'm just
going to leave that one little

00:19:16.160 --> 00:19:18.710
dotted green line and
one little blue line

00:19:18.710 --> 00:19:22.720
to say that this organization
owns all of the projects

00:19:22.720 --> 00:19:27.250
below it and the billing account
pays for all of the projects

00:19:27.250 --> 00:19:29.700
below.

00:19:29.700 --> 00:19:31.770
Now historically,
a GCP organization

00:19:31.770 --> 00:19:36.590
required you to have a
G Suite Apps account.

00:19:36.590 --> 00:19:37.770
It no longer does.

00:19:37.770 --> 00:19:40.050
You can use a free
Cloud Identity account,

00:19:40.050 --> 00:19:45.330
associate that to your
projects in Cloud,

00:19:45.330 --> 00:19:48.300
and use organizations for free
without having to use G Suite.

00:19:48.300 --> 00:19:50.370
So organizations are
available to everyone,

00:19:50.370 --> 00:19:54.270
and they're a fantastic way
to control users, policies,

00:19:54.270 --> 00:19:56.190
permissions at the top level.

00:19:56.190 --> 00:20:00.070
And it's really easy to move
projects into an organization.

00:20:00.070 --> 00:20:03.190
You can also move billing
accounts into an organization

00:20:03.190 --> 00:20:03.690
yourself.

00:20:06.430 --> 00:20:09.562
So we've gotten to
about 10 projects.

00:20:09.562 --> 00:20:11.520
And already, I think you
can see that groupings

00:20:11.520 --> 00:20:17.070
of these projects are
starting to divide themselves.

00:20:17.070 --> 00:20:22.140
We have, on the left, our
development environments,

00:20:22.140 --> 00:20:23.060
production, staging.

00:20:23.060 --> 00:20:24.810
We have, in the middle,
our macro service.

00:20:24.810 --> 00:20:26.672
It had some
environments, as well.

00:20:26.672 --> 00:20:28.380
We've acquired two
companies, apparently.

00:20:28.380 --> 00:20:30.360
Things are really moving.

00:20:30.360 --> 00:20:34.570
TopSecret is still hiding on
the side, along with Archive.

00:20:34.570 --> 00:20:37.510
Folders let you group
projects together.

00:20:37.510 --> 00:20:39.370
Folders can be nested.

00:20:39.370 --> 00:20:42.100
And they also let you
apply, in a more narrow way,

00:20:42.100 --> 00:20:46.660
those policies and settings
that you previously applied

00:20:46.660 --> 00:20:48.580
at the organization level.

00:20:48.580 --> 00:20:50.560
And they are a
fantastic way to start

00:20:50.560 --> 00:20:54.430
thinking about something
like a primary key for how

00:20:54.430 --> 00:20:57.216
you organize billing data.

00:20:57.216 --> 00:20:59.090
Now, a project can only
belong to one folder,

00:20:59.090 --> 00:21:01.700
but of course it can belong
to a nested set of folders.

00:21:01.700 --> 00:21:03.074
So you have to
think a little bit

00:21:03.074 --> 00:21:07.150
ahead of how your folder
structure might represent

00:21:07.150 --> 00:21:10.920
your resources and
your organization.

00:21:10.920 --> 00:21:13.110
Here, we'll chop it
up one way, which

00:21:13.110 --> 00:21:16.560
is to make an app section
for our main application,

00:21:16.560 --> 00:21:21.570
put the macro-services and
their environments in a folder,

00:21:21.570 --> 00:21:24.630
give BizDev their
acquired companies,

00:21:24.630 --> 00:21:30.080
and leave TopSecret and
Archive off to the side.

00:21:30.080 --> 00:21:32.630
And of course, you can go
much further than this.

00:21:32.630 --> 00:21:35.710
You might choose to
cut by department.

00:21:35.710 --> 00:21:38.630
If Finance wants to keep
an area for invoicing

00:21:38.630 --> 00:21:41.960
and a storage area
for vendors, they

00:21:41.960 --> 00:21:43.910
may do that in a
separate folder.

00:21:43.910 --> 00:21:45.290
Of course, you can nest folders.

00:21:45.290 --> 00:21:48.070
Sales has just gone and nested
their folders by region.

00:21:51.270 --> 00:21:54.190
So folders mirror how you work.

00:21:54.190 --> 00:21:57.510
And you'll see them start to
percolate throughout our cost

00:21:57.510 --> 00:22:00.390
management tooling and
throughout our billing

00:22:00.390 --> 00:22:06.950
infrastructure to give you that
view of groups of projects.

00:22:06.950 --> 00:22:10.040
Now, let's get to
the most, I think,

00:22:10.040 --> 00:22:15.950
powerful and interesting
feature of how to control cost,

00:22:15.950 --> 00:22:17.030
and that is the label.

00:22:19.670 --> 00:22:22.130
So you're living in this world.

00:22:22.130 --> 00:22:23.620
You're a CFO.

00:22:23.620 --> 00:22:27.504
And you say, wow, we have
a lot of resources in GCP.

00:22:27.504 --> 00:22:29.170
I heard from a developer
the other day--

00:22:29.170 --> 00:22:30.820
I was having lunch with them.

00:22:30.820 --> 00:22:34.420
And they said that we're
spending 50% of our GCP dollars

00:22:34.420 --> 00:22:35.830
on databases.

00:22:35.830 --> 00:22:37.390
And I said, really?

00:22:37.390 --> 00:22:39.400
I don't see database
up there anywhere.

00:22:39.400 --> 00:22:43.360
How do I know what that
cost is across everything

00:22:43.360 --> 00:22:45.630
that I operate?

00:22:45.630 --> 00:22:47.990
So let's take just a
slice of this world.

00:22:47.990 --> 00:22:49.970
We're going to take
the app section

00:22:49.970 --> 00:22:53.710
and cut away the
rest for simplicity.

00:22:53.710 --> 00:22:58.894
And let's show what's
under the hood.

00:22:58.894 --> 00:23:00.060
We've brought services back.

00:23:00.060 --> 00:23:00.870
We have GCE.

00:23:00.870 --> 00:23:01.890
We have Cloud Storage.

00:23:01.890 --> 00:23:02.850
We have BigQuery.

00:23:02.850 --> 00:23:04.790
We have Cloud SQL.

00:23:04.790 --> 00:23:07.590
And we have a bare
metal database

00:23:07.590 --> 00:23:14.330
running on a bunch of
VMs inside our GCE bucket

00:23:14.330 --> 00:23:16.170
here of resources.

00:23:16.170 --> 00:23:20.330
So we can apply a label
to those resources.

00:23:20.330 --> 00:23:23.650
Now, labels expose themselves
in different ways from service

00:23:23.650 --> 00:23:24.700
to service.

00:23:24.700 --> 00:23:28.300
And their function is not
only for purposes of billing.

00:23:28.300 --> 00:23:31.690
Labels are used for other
reasons, such as in GCE,

00:23:31.690 --> 00:23:37.300
you can filter and sort
and manage VMs by label.

00:23:37.300 --> 00:23:40.080
Now, we've tagged our
bare metal databases here

00:23:40.080 --> 00:23:41.940
with the type database.

00:23:41.940 --> 00:23:45.540
And of course, Cloud SQL, we'll
want to tag those as databases,

00:23:45.540 --> 00:23:46.350
as well.

00:23:46.350 --> 00:23:46.860
But ha ha!

00:23:46.860 --> 00:23:52.210
We realized that we only really
run databases-- a BigQuery

00:23:52.210 --> 00:23:53.290
database in production.

00:23:53.290 --> 00:23:55.480
We don't have that in
the other environments.

00:23:55.480 --> 00:24:00.430
And we back up a whole
huge pile of database data

00:24:00.430 --> 00:24:03.040
into Cloud Storage.

00:24:03.040 --> 00:24:04.510
So let's label that
bucket Database

00:24:04.510 --> 00:24:08.460
as well because it follows
the same cost model.

00:24:08.460 --> 00:24:14.200
Now we have a
cross-cutting view of usage

00:24:14.200 --> 00:24:17.680
that spans all of those
folders and projects,

00:24:17.680 --> 00:24:22.780
and goes across our
entire organization.

00:24:22.780 --> 00:24:27.047
Now, there are trade-offs
between folders and labels.

00:24:27.047 --> 00:24:29.130
And as I mentioned, one
way to think about folders

00:24:29.130 --> 00:24:31.770
is they're kind of a
primary key, a primary index

00:24:31.770 --> 00:24:33.660
across your projects.

00:24:33.660 --> 00:24:38.620
And projects can only
have one parent folder,

00:24:38.620 --> 00:24:41.500
but resources and projects
can have many, many labels.

00:24:41.500 --> 00:24:44.110
As I showed before, it's
common for customers

00:24:44.110 --> 00:24:47.470
to have dozens of labels
on complex resources

00:24:47.470 --> 00:24:49.990
that tell them all about
the environment, location,

00:24:49.990 --> 00:24:54.920
customer type, and other
metadata about their resources.

00:24:54.920 --> 00:24:58.022
Now our guidance here
is be deliberate.

00:24:58.022 --> 00:24:59.730
Now, you have to be
especially deliberate

00:24:59.730 --> 00:25:03.000
when you're constructing
folders because you

00:25:03.000 --> 00:25:04.680
have to build a hierarchy.

00:25:04.680 --> 00:25:06.630
But with labels,
it's a good idea

00:25:06.630 --> 00:25:08.250
to be deliberate, as well--

00:25:08.250 --> 00:25:13.200
define the keys that are
mandatory across services,

00:25:13.200 --> 00:25:18.350
define what valid values are,
and set a policy around which

00:25:18.350 --> 00:25:22.410
kinds of services require
labeling and which don't.

00:25:22.410 --> 00:25:25.200
And as I mentioned,
some services

00:25:25.200 --> 00:25:27.060
use labels for other functions.

00:25:27.060 --> 00:25:30.180
Some services also
apply their own labels

00:25:30.180 --> 00:25:32.610
to resources for
your benefit when

00:25:32.610 --> 00:25:35.740
you're looking at BigQuery
export in other places.

00:25:35.740 --> 00:25:37.620
So you'll get cluster IDs.

00:25:37.620 --> 00:25:41.400
Deployment Manager will
append IDs onto its resources.

00:25:41.400 --> 00:25:43.470
And you'll see those
percolate all the way

00:25:43.470 --> 00:25:47.176
throughout cost reporting
and throughout BigQuery.

00:25:47.176 --> 00:25:48.550
Now, how does this
actually look?

00:25:48.550 --> 00:25:52.840
Here is a slice of
a BigQuery export.

00:25:52.840 --> 00:25:56.290
If you had a pool of VMs
that are reporting cost

00:25:56.290 --> 00:26:01.390
through to BigQuery, we
are going to cut those up

00:26:01.390 --> 00:26:03.590
by labeled usage.

00:26:03.590 --> 00:26:07.300
So you'll get a line item for
each combination of labels

00:26:07.300 --> 00:26:09.740
and the associated cost.

00:26:09.740 --> 00:26:12.430
And one of the great
powers of BigQuery

00:26:12.430 --> 00:26:15.970
is its ability to query
into nested data structures.

00:26:15.970 --> 00:26:18.010
So we provide you example
queries and example

00:26:18.010 --> 00:26:22.300
ways of surfacing these
labels in our BigQuery export

00:26:22.300 --> 00:26:27.670
documentation that tell you and
let you pivot on those values

00:26:27.670 --> 00:26:32.160
to do real fine
cost attribution.

00:26:32.160 --> 00:26:35.340
So use labels for
cross-cutting reporting

00:26:35.340 --> 00:26:37.680
that span all of your projects.

00:26:37.680 --> 00:26:38.710
And they're powerful.

00:26:38.710 --> 00:26:41.880
We have customers who use
them as their primary means

00:26:41.880 --> 00:26:44.160
of managing cost and don't
use folders as heavily.

00:26:44.160 --> 00:26:45.330
It depends on your workload.

00:26:45.330 --> 00:26:51.360
And it depends how flexibly you
want to see the world of cost.

00:26:51.360 --> 00:26:53.430
I will caution you
on one note, which

00:26:53.430 --> 00:26:56.580
is that most of our labels
are evaluated at the time

00:26:56.580 --> 00:26:58.390
that BigQuery export runs.

00:26:58.390 --> 00:27:01.110
So if you're applying labels
in a really high frequency way

00:27:01.110 --> 00:27:03.330
to underlying
resources, your export

00:27:03.330 --> 00:27:07.390
may not exactly match
those in perfect real time.

00:27:07.390 --> 00:27:10.020
So when I say high frequency,
I mean if you're putting labels

00:27:10.020 --> 00:27:13.920
on and off resources at an
inter-hour or a really fine

00:27:13.920 --> 00:27:16.360
rate, much faster than
our BigQuery export,

00:27:16.360 --> 00:27:18.780
which is a few
hours delayed, you

00:27:18.780 --> 00:27:20.796
may see the last
value of that label.

00:27:20.796 --> 00:27:22.170
So it's worth
experimenting with.

00:27:22.170 --> 00:27:24.940
And if you have
needs that require

00:27:24.940 --> 00:27:30.120
real fine grain labeling in
real time, come talk to us.

00:27:30.120 --> 00:27:32.872
Reach out to the billing product
management or engineering

00:27:32.872 --> 00:27:35.330
teams, and we'll be happy to
talk to you about those cases.

00:27:38.720 --> 00:27:40.200
Now, a few advanced topics.

00:27:40.200 --> 00:27:42.470
And I think we'll have about
10 minutes for questions

00:27:42.470 --> 00:27:45.880
after we get through advanced
topics and our summary.

00:27:45.880 --> 00:27:48.780
And we'll have two microphones
ready for you for any questions

00:27:48.780 --> 00:27:49.280
you have.

00:27:49.280 --> 00:27:53.390
So please start thinking
of them in your mind.

00:27:53.390 --> 00:27:58.300
First, billing roles
and permissions.

00:27:58.300 --> 00:28:02.980
Billing permissions are separate
from resource permissions.

00:28:02.980 --> 00:28:06.880
What I mean by that is
you have a series of roles

00:28:06.880 --> 00:28:08.500
that you can centrally
control if you

00:28:08.500 --> 00:28:10.240
have an organization
on the billing

00:28:10.240 --> 00:28:12.430
accounts in that organization.

00:28:12.430 --> 00:28:16.840
Some of those common roles are
the billing administrator role,

00:28:16.840 --> 00:28:20.090
who has the rights
to do everything,

00:28:20.090 --> 00:28:22.690
a billing account creator,
who can create other billing

00:28:22.690 --> 00:28:25.690
accounts, and a
billing account viewer,

00:28:25.690 --> 00:28:28.990
who can look at
cost and reporting,

00:28:28.990 --> 00:28:30.820
but who cannot
change the settings,

00:28:30.820 --> 00:28:32.770
change the way
payments are processed,

00:28:32.770 --> 00:28:37.160
change how your
finances are run.

00:28:37.160 --> 00:28:40.800
Now, once your GCP
organization is established,

00:28:40.800 --> 00:28:42.650
there are a couple
of best practices

00:28:42.650 --> 00:28:44.570
around billing
account permissions

00:28:44.570 --> 00:28:47.100
that we want you to
take home with you.

00:28:47.100 --> 00:28:50.570
The first is, after
you've created your org,

00:28:50.570 --> 00:28:54.400
if you're a preexisting GCP
customer, move your billing

00:28:54.400 --> 00:28:56.740
accounts into the org.

00:28:56.740 --> 00:28:58.490
That doesn't happen
for you automatically.

00:28:58.490 --> 00:29:00.573
There's a big button on
top of the billing account

00:29:00.573 --> 00:29:03.020
that says to choose
the organization it's

00:29:03.020 --> 00:29:04.250
associated to.

00:29:04.250 --> 00:29:07.580
So you can move them and gain
that same level of control

00:29:07.580 --> 00:29:09.920
you have after you've
moved all of your projects

00:29:09.920 --> 00:29:13.290
into the organization,
which is the second tip.

00:29:13.290 --> 00:29:17.010
The third is once you have
your billing structure set up,

00:29:17.010 --> 00:29:19.410
you can remove the
billing account creator

00:29:19.410 --> 00:29:22.950
role that exists as a
default at the top level

00:29:22.950 --> 00:29:24.660
of the organization.

00:29:24.660 --> 00:29:28.800
It's there to allow the easy
adoption of organizations,

00:29:28.800 --> 00:29:31.020
but once you've got your
structure nailed down,

00:29:31.020 --> 00:29:32.450
you may well want
to take it away.

00:29:37.180 --> 00:29:40.270
A couple of best practices
on billing account hygiene.

00:29:40.270 --> 00:29:42.580
All things equal,
we generally think

00:29:42.580 --> 00:29:46.330
that fewer billing accounts
are preferable to many,

00:29:46.330 --> 00:29:48.580
unless you have specific
reasons for having

00:29:48.580 --> 00:29:51.520
lots of billing accounts.

00:29:51.520 --> 00:29:54.762
Costs always flow up to
a single billing account.

00:29:54.762 --> 00:29:56.470
And you can think of
that billing account

00:29:56.470 --> 00:30:00.950
as the top level container
for cost reporting.

00:30:00.950 --> 00:30:05.140
So if you have usage spread
across billing accounts,

00:30:05.140 --> 00:30:08.890
you may have to construct
reports in less easy ways

00:30:08.890 --> 00:30:12.400
to see costs between
those billing accounts.

00:30:12.400 --> 00:30:14.710
Some of the reasons
you may want or need

00:30:14.710 --> 00:30:16.140
multiple billing accounts.

00:30:16.140 --> 00:30:18.460
If you have divisions
of a large corporation,

00:30:18.460 --> 00:30:20.330
they may want their
own control of costs.

00:30:20.330 --> 00:30:22.450
They may operate in
their own currency.

00:30:22.450 --> 00:30:24.760
As I mentioned before,
a billing account

00:30:24.760 --> 00:30:27.460
operates in a single currency.

00:30:27.460 --> 00:30:30.040
And there may be
other reasons that you

00:30:30.040 --> 00:30:33.070
want to split charges or
split invoicing in a way that

00:30:33.070 --> 00:30:35.712
follow a different path.

00:30:35.712 --> 00:30:38.170
Above all, if you're thinking
you're going down that route,

00:30:38.170 --> 00:30:40.540
talk to your account
manager or I'm

00:30:40.540 --> 00:30:42.430
happy to answer questions after.

00:30:42.430 --> 00:30:44.370
But generally speaking,
fewer is better.

00:30:48.290 --> 00:30:52.000
Finally, you may have a
use case for charge-backs

00:30:52.000 --> 00:30:54.310
internally inside
your organization,

00:30:54.310 --> 00:30:55.910
where you have one
billing account,

00:30:55.910 --> 00:30:58.150
but where you're
allocating costs

00:30:58.150 --> 00:31:02.400
in a more discreet manner
inside your company.

00:31:02.400 --> 00:31:05.940
And there are different
ways to cut charge-backs.

00:31:05.940 --> 00:31:07.590
You can do it by folder.

00:31:07.590 --> 00:31:09.180
You can do it by label.

00:31:09.180 --> 00:31:10.980
You can do it by project.

00:31:10.980 --> 00:31:13.890
We'd love to help with that
problem, so by all means,

00:31:13.890 --> 00:31:15.870
please reach out and
talk to us, and we're

00:31:15.870 --> 00:31:18.659
happy to give you some
idea of best practice

00:31:18.659 --> 00:31:20.700
and give you an idea of
what other customers have

00:31:20.700 --> 00:31:22.870
done before you with
our reporting tools.

00:31:27.420 --> 00:31:29.980
Finally, a note-- I
mentioned it earlier.

00:31:29.980 --> 00:31:33.520
There is a dedicated Cloud
Billing Support team.

00:31:33.520 --> 00:31:36.840
Whether you pay for Google
Cloud Support or not,

00:31:36.840 --> 00:31:40.032
this team is always available
to you to answer questions.

00:31:40.032 --> 00:31:41.490
And in the support
section, there's

00:31:41.490 --> 00:31:45.390
a clear link that says where to
reach out to the Cloud Billing

00:31:45.390 --> 00:31:47.220
Support team.

00:31:47.220 --> 00:31:48.990
And as I've hammered
home, they will

00:31:48.990 --> 00:31:51.892
be extra excited if you turned
on your BigQuery billing export

00:31:51.892 --> 00:31:53.850
because they'd love to
get down in and help you

00:31:53.850 --> 00:31:56.820
with some of those cost
attribution questions

00:31:56.820 --> 00:32:00.960
at the fine level of detail.

00:32:00.960 --> 00:32:04.770
So a quick summary, some
ways to learn more, and then

00:32:04.770 --> 00:32:08.070
about 10 minutes for questions.

00:32:08.070 --> 00:32:11.580
First-- you've already
heard me say that enough--

00:32:11.580 --> 00:32:14.380
turn it on.

00:32:14.380 --> 00:32:17.380
Projects are a great way
to enforce boundaries,

00:32:17.380 --> 00:32:19.660
and you can create them freely.

00:32:19.660 --> 00:32:22.420
Use projects for any
use case you see fit.

00:32:25.340 --> 00:32:30.440
Prefer cost reporting to relying
on the invoice for details

00:32:30.440 --> 00:32:31.910
about cost.

00:32:31.910 --> 00:32:34.700
The invoice is a great tool
for that quick summary,

00:32:34.700 --> 00:32:38.420
but cost reporting lets you get
down to slicing and dicing cost

00:32:38.420 --> 00:32:41.350
in all of the ways
we've talked about.

00:32:41.350 --> 00:32:42.967
GCP Orgs are for everyone.

00:32:42.967 --> 00:32:44.800
They're a fantastic way
to control resources

00:32:44.800 --> 00:32:46.660
if you don't already
have one, and you

00:32:46.660 --> 00:32:49.390
can set them up either
using Cloud IDaaS

00:32:49.390 --> 00:32:52.470
or using your G Suite
applications domain.

00:32:55.030 --> 00:32:57.490
Folders, which you can nest
up to four levels deep,

00:32:57.490 --> 00:33:01.120
are a great way to mirror the
structure of how you work,

00:33:01.120 --> 00:33:05.530
whether it's by team, by
department, by project,

00:33:05.530 --> 00:33:08.627
with how your resources
sit in Google Cloud.

00:33:08.627 --> 00:33:10.960
And you can think of them
something like the primary key

00:33:10.960 --> 00:33:16.390
or the primary way to
index your projects.

00:33:16.390 --> 00:33:20.190
And finally, labels are a way
to supercharge reporting and cut

00:33:20.190 --> 00:33:22.980
across the boundaries
of projects.

00:33:22.980 --> 00:33:24.420
Create as many as you like.

00:33:24.420 --> 00:33:27.630
Use them for those cases where
you need to slice and dice

00:33:27.630 --> 00:33:32.040
in ways that folders can't
do or folders can't do alone.

00:33:32.040 --> 00:33:34.410
And as I said, Cloud Billing
Support is here to help.

00:33:34.410 --> 00:33:36.480
We've heard from
lots of customers.

00:33:36.480 --> 00:33:39.829
We talk to customers every day
about the different problems

00:33:39.829 --> 00:33:41.370
they're aiming to
solve with billing.

00:33:41.370 --> 00:33:46.850
And we're thrilled to help
and find solutions any time.

00:33:46.850 --> 00:33:52.070
As I mentioned, there
are four more sessions--

